  </div>

  <!-- Engineering Science & Physics Deep Dive -->
  <div class="bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-7 mb-10 shadow-xs">
    <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-4 mb-6">
      <h2 class="text-title-md font-semibold text-ink">
        {d.scienceTitle}
      </h2>
      <p class="text-xs text-muted mt-1">
        {d.scienceSub}
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>⚡</span>
          <span>1. Modified Paschen-Townsend Breakdown in Low-Pressure Micro-Plasmas</span>
        </h3>
        <p>
          In high-frequency electromagnetic fields, the dielectric breakdown threshold of rarefied gases is described by the microwave modified Townsend breakdown criterion:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 text-purple-300 p-3 rounded-lg font-mono text-[11px]">
          E_breakdown = B &bull; p &bull; [1 + (&omega; / &nu;_m)&sup2;]^(1/2)
        </div>
        <p>
          where <em>p</em> is chamber pressure (mbar), <em>&omega;</em> is angular generator frequency (2&pi; &bull; 2.45 GHz &approx; 1.54 &times; 10¹⁰ rad/s), and <em>&nu;_m</em> is the electron-neutral momentum collision frequency (&nu;_m &approx; 5.3 &times; 10⁹ &bull; p s⁻¹). At pressures between 20 and 50 mbar, &omega; &approx; &nu;_m, driving the Townsend breakdown threshold to an absolute minimum (<em>E_min</em> &approx; 250 to 350 V/cm). If local electric fields surpass this threshold, spontaneous glowing ionization flashover occurs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>🌊</span>
          <span>2. Dielectric Loss Factor Volumetric Power Dissipation &amp; Dry-Spot Collapse</span>
        </h3>
        <p>
          Microwave heating power absorbed per cubic meter of food extract follows Lambert's law of volumetric dielectric dissipation:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 text-purple-300 p-3 rounded-lg font-mono text-[11px]">
          P_v = 2 &bull; &pi; &bull; f &bull; &epsilon;_0 &bull; &epsilon;&Prime; &bull; E_rms&sup2;
        </div>
        <p>
          where <em>&epsilon;_0</em> is vacuum permittivity (8.854 &times; 10⁻¹² F/m) and <em>&epsilon;&Prime;</em> is the material's dielectric loss factor. As moisture evaporates from 65% down to &le;4%, &epsilon;&Prime; drops abruptly from 18.5 to &lt;0.5. With the dielectric absorbing load removed, cavity Q-factor spikes, causing standing wave electric field <em>E_rms</em> to double, concentrating energy onto remaining damp clusters and triggering explosive scorching within seconds.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>🔄</span>
          <span>3. Ferrite Circulator Impedance Mismatch &amp; Reflected Wave Isolation</span>
        </h3>
        <p>
          The microwave reflection coefficient (&Gamma;) and reflected power returned toward the generator are governed by load impedance mismatch:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 text-purple-300 p-3 rounded-lg font-mono text-[11px]">
          &Gamma; = (Z_load - Z_0) / (Z_load + Z_0) &emsp;|&emsp; P_refl = P_fwd &bull; |&Gamma;|&sup2;
        </div>
        <p>
          In a 3-port junction ferrite circulator, anisotropic magnetized ferrites rotate microwave polarization, directing forward power from Port 1 (magnetron) to Port 2 (vacuum cavity), while redirecting reflected power from Port 2 exclusively into Port 3 (deionized water dummy load) with &ge;25 dB isolation, keeping reflected cathode return below 0.3%.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>🔬</span>
          <span>4. Vacuum Saturation Flash Boiling &amp; Wagner Vapor Pressure Kinetics</span>
        </h3>
        <p>
          Moisture boiling temperature inside the vacuum vessel is determined by the Clausius-Clapeyron relation via the simplified Wagner saturation equation:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 text-purple-300 p-3 rounded-lg font-mono text-[11px]">
          T_sat (&deg;C) &approx; 100 &bull; (p_mbar / 1013.25)^(0.19) - 0.5
        </div>
        <p>
          At 35 mbar, water flashes into vapor at exactly 26.8°C (80.2°F). Latent heat of vaporization (2,410 kJ/kg) cools the product bed continuously, shielding heat-sensitive phytochemicals (anthocyanins, polyphenols, vitamins) from thermal degradation as long as free moisture is present.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Engineering Specifications Comparison Table -->
